+\subsection*{Recovering the finite tiles}\label{subsec:ordered_identification}
+Suppose that the assembled function is $P$ on $S$ and that the incoming translated copy is $Q$ on $B$. Lower the incoming copy by
+\[
+       \delta=\min_{x\in S\cap B}\bigl(Q(x)-P(x)\bigr).
+\]
+Keep $P$ on $S$ and assign $Q-\delta$ on $B\setminus S$. This is the pointwise minimum on the intersection. Suppose $P=T+c$ on $S$. To identify the result on $S\cup B$, it suffices to find a scalar $b$ such that $Q-b=T+c$ on $B\setminus S$, $Q-b\ge T+c$ on the intersection, and equality holds at one point of the intersection. Then $\delta=b$ and the result is $T+c$ throughout $S\cup B$. A change of constant on a completed correction is absorbed by the same rule at its next insertion.
+
+Apply this observation in the insertion order of Section~\ref{subsec:full_recursion}. Part II establishes the three comparisons just stated for the actual translated sources, including a point of equality with the already assembled portion at each insertion. An ordinary standard uses six copies. An ordinary alternate also uses one earlier alternate. A corrected output has four ordinary copies and two $L$-corrections. Each correction uses copies of one earlier standard, a second standard at the join, and copies of an earlier alternate. The elongated correction uses one more copy of that alternate. Both lengths use the ancestors saved before the new digit is processed.
+
+There is an instance where the weak inequality is essential. For the reciprocal pure ancestor with vector $(e,1)$, $e\ge3$ odd, put $Y=(1-e,e+1)$. In the right correction with run parameter $k+1$ the first alternate is one unit above the preceding standard at $kY+(0,1)$ and $kY+(0,2)$. The preceding standard gives the required value at both points. The incoming copy attains equality elsewhere on the overlap and has the required value at every new point. Part II checks that these are exactly the two exceptions in this orientation and gives the corresponding reflected placements. For proper ancestors the comparisons are with the common minimum on the actual incoming domain. The comparison for the \emph{current} alternate is made on its own domain, where that minimum equals its rounded field. Thus induction, first through the pieces of a correction and then through the pieces of the tile, identifies the entire finite tile with the appropriate field up to one additive constant.
+
+\subsection*{Extension, growth and recurrence}
+\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:global_odometers}
+For each reduced fraction $0<n/d<1$ the standard and alternate tile odometers extend to functions $g_{n/d},\hat g_{n/d}$, respectively, satisfying \eqref{eq:periodicity}. For each of these functions $f$ there is a $b\in\R^2$ for which
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:periodicity_growth}
+     f(x)-\tfrac12 x^TM(n,d)x-b^Tx
+\end{equation}
+is $L'(n,d)$-periodic.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Use the field identified with the finite tile and choose its additive constant to agree there. Part III proves that the actual finite tile meets every class of $L'(n,d)$, for each of the four outputs separately. Its induction uses the following cases.
+\[
+\begin{array}{c|cccc}
+\text{last digit}&\text{standard odd}&\text{standard even}
+                 &\text{alternate odd}&\text{alternate even}\\ \hline
+1&\text{ordinary}&\text{ordinary}&\text{ordinary}&\text{ordinary}\\
+2&\text{ordinary}&\text{short }L&\text{long }L&\text{ordinary}\\
+3&\text{short }L&\text{ordinary}&\text{ordinary}&\text{long }L
+\end{array}
+\]
+The induction hypothesis is coverage by the earlier standard and alternate \emph{tiles}. The short and long cases use the actual finite pieces of the correction. The empty word and pure runs use their explicit domains. Coverage together with the height identification shows that translating the finite output with the field's period constants assigns a consistent value at every site.
+
+Explicitly, put $U=\mu(d,n)$, $V=(n-d,n+d)$, $a=(n,-d)$ and $T=d^2+2dn-n^2$, where $\mu=2$ for odd $n+d$ and $1$ otherwise. Then $M U=0$, $M V=a$, $a\cdot U=0$ and $a\cdot V=-T$. The two generator increments give, for every $i,j\in\Z$,
+\[
+ f(x+iU+jV)=f(x)+i c_U+j(a\cdot x+c_V)-T\,j(j-1)/2 .
+\]
+The two translations commute. Choose $b$ by $b\cdot v=c_v-\tfrac12 v^TMv$ for $v=U,V$. The independence of $U,V$ determines $b$, and subtraction proves the claimed periodicity. Boundedness on a period cell gives \eqref{eq:quadratic_growth}.
+\end{proof}
