+\begin{lemma}
+For each $0<n/d<1$, the Laplacians of $g_{n/d}$ and $\hat g_{n/d}$ take values in $\{-1,0\}$.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+The standard seam comparisons and the midpoint calculation give the binary Laplacians of the whole-plane fields. Lemma~\ref{lemma:global_odometers} identifies the extensions of the finite tiles with those fields on every incoming star.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{lemma}
+For each $0<n/d<1$, $g_{n/d}$ and $\hat g_{n/d}$ are recurrent.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Use the standard rank and the lexicographic alternate rank, transported by the parity-correct quarter-turn for the odd type. The least-rank argument excludes every nonempty finite forbidden set for each of the two fields. Apply Proposition~\ref{prop:recurrence}.
+\end{proof}
+
+To finish Theorem~\ref{theorem:odometers}, distinguish the two functions.
+For an even fraction, translation by $U$ leaves $H-F$ unchanged. Translation by $V$ changes it by $-1$, since $a\cdot\sigma=-2$ in the midpoint formula. An integer affine function with zero increment on $(d,n)$ has gradient $m(n,-d)$ for $m\in\Z$. Its $V$ increment is $-mT$, which cannot be $-1$ at an interior fraction. For odd type the quarter-turn takes the doubled kernel period $U=2(d,n)$ to a signed transverse period of the even partner. In the reflected orientation use the standard's reflection identity before comparing with the alternate. The two standard placements have the same kernel increment, and the difference of the alternate and standard has $U$ increment $+1$ or $-1$. The literal odd initializers have increment $-1$ (part II, \emph{Period increments and distinctness}). An integer affine function has even increment on $2(d,n)$. This proves the asserted distinctness in the fixed coordinates of \eqref{eq:periodicity}.
+
+
+In particular the two Laplacians differ in these fixed coordinates. Otherwise the difference of the two odometers would be harmonic and would have constant increments on $U,V$. Subtract a real linear function with those increments. The result is periodic and harmonic, hence constant by the maximum principle on the finite strongly connected quotient. The odometer difference would then be affine with integer gradient, which the preceding period computation excludes.
